<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-stokes-theorem" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
<title>Stokes' Theorem</title>

<introduction>
<p>
The circulation-curl form of Green's theorem,
<xref ref="thm-green-tangential"/>, traded a circulation around a closed
curve in the plane for a double integral of
<m>(\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F}) \cdot \mathbf{k}</m> over the region
the curve encloses. Nothing in that statement really needs the region to
be flat. If we bend the region up out of the plane into a surface
<m>S</m> in space, keeping <m>C</m> as its boundary, the same exchange
still works: the circulation of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> around <m>C</m> equals
the flux of the curl of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> through <m>S</m>. That is
<term>Stokes' theorem</term>, the promise made in the footnote of
<xref ref="sec-greens-theorem-forms"/>.
</p>

<p>
It completes the family. <xref ref="section-divergence-theorem"/> turned a
flux integral over a <em>closed</em> surface into a triple integral of the
divergence; Stokes' theorem turns a line integral around the
<em>boundary</em> of an open surface into a surface integral of the curl.
Both say the same kind of thing: what happens on the boundary of a piece
is the accumulation, over the piece, of a derivative of the field.
</p>
</introduction>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-curl-3d">
<title>Curl in Three Dimensions</title>

<p>
In <xref ref="sec-curl"/> we only needed the component of the curl along
<m>\mathbf{k}</m>, because in the plane a paddle wheel can only spin about
an axis perpendicular to the page. In space the axis can point anywhere,
so the curl is a vector: it points along the axis the paddle wheel spins
about, and its length is the circulation density about that axis.
</p>

<definition xml:id="def-curl-3d">
<statement>
<p>
The <term>curl</term> of the vector field
<m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j} + P\,\mathbf{k}</m>, whose
components have continuous first partial derivatives, is
</p>

<md>
<mrow xml:id="eq-curl-3d" number="yes">
\operatorname{curl} \mathbf{F} = \nabla \times \mathbf{F}
\amp = \begin{vmatrix}
\mathbf{i} \amp \mathbf{j} \amp \mathbf{k} \\
\dfrac{\partial}{\partial x} \amp \dfrac{\partial}{\partial y}
\amp \dfrac{\partial}{\partial z} \\
M \amp N \amp P
\end{vmatrix}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \left( \frac{\partial P}{\partial y}
- \frac{\partial N}{\partial z} \right)\mathbf{i}
+ \left( \frac{\partial M}{\partial z}
- \frac{\partial P}{\partial x} \right)\mathbf{j}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \qquad + \left( \frac{\partial N}{\partial x}
- \frac{\partial M}{\partial y} \right)\mathbf{k}.
</mrow>
</md>
</statement>
</definition>

<p>
The <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of <xref ref="eq-curl-3d"/> is exactly the
quantity <m>\dfrac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \dfrac{\partial M}{\partial y}</m>
of <xref ref="eq-curl-def"/>, so the two-dimensional curl of
<xref ref="sec-curl"/> was one component of this vector all along.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-curl-3d">
<title>Computing a Curl</title>
<statement>
<p>
Find <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m> for
<m>\mathbf{F} = xz\,\mathbf{i} + xyz\,\mathbf{j} - y^2\,\mathbf{k}</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-curl-3d">
<p>
Here <m>M = xz</m>, <m>N = xyz</m>, and <m>P = -y^2</m>. Taking the
three components of <xref ref="eq-curl-3d"/> in turn,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial P}{\partial y} - \frac{\partial N}{\partial z}
\amp = -2y - xy,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial M}{\partial z} - \frac{\partial P}{\partial x}
\amp = x - 0 = x,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\frac{\partial N}{\partial x} - \frac{\partial M}{\partial y}
\amp = yz - 0 = yz,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
so that
</p>

<me>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F}
= -(2y + xy)\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j} + yz\,\mathbf{k}.
</me>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-curl-paddle-tikz">
<caption>
The curl of <xref ref="example-curl-3d"/> at the point <m>P(1,1,1)</m>,
where <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = -3\,\mathbf{i} + \mathbf{j} +
\mathbf{k}</m>. The curl is the axis of the paddle wheel: put the wheel in
the plane perpendicular to it and the field turns the wheel the way the
curved arrow shows: counterclockwise as seen from the tip of the curl, by
the right-hand rule.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-curl-paddle" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A vector at a point in space with a small paddle wheel turning about it.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
At the point P equals one, one, one, located by dashed guide lines from
the x and y axes, a green arrow points in the direction of the curl, up and
back toward the negative x direction. A small vermillion disc with four
blades, the paddle wheel, sits in the plane perpendicular to that arrow, and
a curved vermillion arrow beside its rim shows which way it turns:
counterclockwise as seen from the tip of the green arrow.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The curl of F = <xz, xyz, -y^2> at P(1,1,1), which is <-3, 1, 1>, as the axis of a
// paddle wheel; the wheel turns counterclockwise seen from the tip of the curl.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(11cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={118}{20}).
triple cam = (0.8297, 0.4412, 0.3420);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-1.2, 0, 0)--(2.4, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -0.6, 0)--(0, 2.6, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 0, 2.6), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

triple P = (1, 1, 1);
triple n = unit((-3, 1, 1));             // the direction of the curl
// e1, e2 span the plane of the wheel, with e1 x e2 = n (right-handed about n)
triple e1 = unit(cross(n, Z)), e2 = cross(n, e1);
real R = 0.6;

// where P is: dashed guides from the axes
pen guide = gray(0.55) + linewidth(0.6pt) + linetype("4 3");
draw((1, 0, 0)--(1, 1, 0)--(0, 1, 0), guide);
draw((1, 1, 0)--P, guide);

// the paddle wheel: a disc in the plane perpendicular to the curl, with four blades
triple wheel(real t) { return P + R*(cos(t)*e1 + sin(t)*e2); }
path3 rim = graph(wheel, 0, 2pi, 72) & cycle;
draw(surface(rim), surfacepen=material(0.3*oivermillion + 0.7*white + opacity(0.45),
                                       emissivepen=0.25*oivermillion, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(rim, oivermillion + linewidth(1.3pt));
for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) draw(P--wheel(pi/4 + k*pi/2), oivermillion + linewidth(1.2pt));

// the way it turns: from e1 towards e2, the right-hand rule about the curl
triple turn(real t) { return P + 1.28*R*(cos(t)*e1 + sin(t)*e2); }
draw(graph(turn, 2.0, 3.6, 24), oivermillion + linewidth(1.5pt), Arrow3(size=12));
label("paddle wheel", wheel(0.55) + 0.25*e1, E, oivermillion + fontsize(13pt));

// the curl, the axis of the wheel
draw(P--P + 1.45*n, oigreen + linewidth(1.7pt), Arrow3(size=10));
label("$\nabla\times\mathbf{F}$", P + 1.45*n, N, oigreen + fontsize(16pt));
dot(P, linewidth(4.5pt));
// its name stands beside the wheel, joined to P by a thin leader, so that the wheel cannot hide it
triple L = P + (0.55, -0.85, -0.1);
draw(L--P, gray(0.4) + linewidth(0.5pt));
label("$P(1,1,1)$", L, W, fontsize(13pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-stokes-theorem">
<title>Stokes' Theorem</title>

<p>
Stokes' theorem needs the orientation of the surface and the direction of
travel along its boundary to agree. The rule is the
<term>right-hand rule</term>: if the thumb of your right hand points along
the unit normal <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, your fingers curl in the positive
direction along the boundary curve <m>C</m>. Equivalently, walking along
<m>C</m> with your head pointing in the direction of
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, the surface is on your left. This is the
orientation drawn in <xref ref="fig-stokes-orientation-tikz"/>; reversing
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> reverses the direction on <m>C</m> and changes the
sign of both sides of the theorem.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-stokes">
<title>Stokes' Theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>S</m> be a piecewise smooth oriented surface with unit normal
field <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, whose boundary is a piecewise smooth
simple closed curve <m>C</m> traversed in the direction given by the
right-hand rule. Let <m>\mathbf{F}</m> be a vector field whose
components have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region
containing <m>S</m>. Then the circulation of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> around
<m>C</m> equals the flux of <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m> through
<m>S</m>:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-stokes">
\underbrace{\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}}_{\text{circulation
around the boundary } C}
= \underbrace{\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
\hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma}_{\text{flux of the curl through } S}.
</men>
</statement>
</theorem>

<remark xml:id="remark-stokes-green">
<p>
Green's theorem is the flat case. If <m>S</m> is a region <m>R</m> of the
<m>xy</m>-plane with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{k}</m>, then
<m>d\sigma = dx\,dy</m> and
<m>(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \mathbf{k}</m> is the
<m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of the curl, so <xref ref="eq-stokes"/>
becomes <xref ref="eq-green-tangential"/> word for word.
</p>
</remark>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-orientation-tikz">
<caption>
The orientation in <xref ref="thm-stokes"/>. The unit normal
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> and the direction of travel along the boundary
<m>C</m> are related by the right-hand rule: with the thumb along
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, the fingers curl the way <m>C</m> is traversed.
The small arrow circling <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> shows that curl of the
fingers.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-stokes-orientation" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A curved cap of a surface in space with its boundary circle, arrows
along the boundary, and a normal vector standing on the cap.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent blue cap, like a piece of a sphere, sits above the
horizontal plane. Its boundary is a closed curve drawn in vermillion
with arrowheads showing the direction of travel, counterclockwise seen
from above. A green arrow labeled n hat rises from a point of the cap
off the axis, perpendicular to the cap there, and a small green arrow
circles it counterclockwise. With the right thumb along that normal, the
fingers curl the way the small arrow and the boundary arrows point.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The orientation in the Stokes theorem: a cap S, its boundary C traversed counterclockwise
// seen from above, and the unit normal n-hat that the right-hand rule pairs with it.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(11.5cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={120}{24}).
triple cam = (0.7912, 0.4568, 0.4067);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// arrowheads along a closed curve: short pieces of it, each ending in a head
void heads(triple f(real), real[] at, pen p, real len=0.22) {
  for (real t : at) draw(graph(f, t - len, t, 12), p, Arrow3(size=13));
}

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-1.6, 0, 0)--(1.6, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -1.6, 0)--(0, 1.6, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 0, 2.0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the cap z = 1.35 - 0.45 (x^2 + y^2) over the unit disk
triple cap(pair q) { return (q.x*cos(q.y), q.x*sin(q.y), 1.35 - 0.45*q.x^2); }
draw(surface(cap, (0, 0), (1, 2pi), 6, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.4*oiblue + 0.6*white + opacity(0.6),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
pen mesh = 0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + linewidth(0.4pt);
for (real r : new real[] {1/3, 2/3})
  draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return cap((r, t)); }, 0, 2pi, 60), mesh);
for (int k = 0; k < 8; ++k)
  draw(graph(new triple(real r) { return cap((r, k*pi/4)); }, 0, 1, 12), mesh);

// the boundary C, counterclockwise seen from above
triple C(real t) { return (cos(t), sin(t), 0.9); }
draw(graph(C, 0, 2pi, 120), oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));
heads(C, new real[] {0.45, 2.55, 4.65}, oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("$C$", C(0.95) + 0.22*(cos(0.95), sin(0.95), 0), oivermillion + fontsize(18pt));

// the unit normal, planted off the axis so that it is not taken for the z-axis
// (on the side of the cap that the viewer sees in profile)
real br = 0.6, bt = 1.95;
triple base = cap((br, bt));
triple n = unit((0.9*br*cos(bt), 0.9*br*sin(bt), 1));
draw(base--base + 0.8*n, oigreen + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
dot(base, oigreen + linewidth(4pt));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", base + 0.8*n, NE, oigreen + fontsize(18pt));
// the way the fingers curl about the thumb n-hat: the same way C is traversed
triple e1 = unit(cross(n, X)), e2 = cross(n, e1);
triple curlarc(real t) { return base + 0.42*n + 0.17*(cos(t)*e1 + sin(t)*e2); }
draw(graph(curlarc, 0.6, 5.6, 40), oigreen + linewidth(1.1pt), Arrow3(size=7));

label("$S$", cap((0.6, -0.9)) + toviewer, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Why is the theorem true? The animation in
<xref ref="fig-stokes-proof-idea-video"/> tells the story without any
algebra. Chop <m>S</m> into tiny, nearly flat patches; on each one,
<m>(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> is the
circulation of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> per unit area, so the patch contributes
<m>(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,\Delta\sigma</m> of
circulation. Add up the patches: every interior edge is shared by two of
them and travelled in opposite directions, so the interior cancels
completely and only the boundary <m>C</m> survives. Shrinking the patches
turns the sum into the flux integral, and <xref ref="eq-stokes"/> falls
out.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-proof-idea-video">
<caption>
The ideas behind the proof of Stokes' theorem, for
<m>\mathbf{F} = \langle M,N,P \rangle</m>:
<m>(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> is circulation
per unit area, the surface is chopped into tiny, nearly flat patches,
every interior edge is travelled twice in opposite directions and
cancels, and only the boundary <m>C</m> survives. A flat surface
recovers Green's theorem, as in <xref ref="remark-stokes-green"/>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-stokes-proof-idea" width="85%"
       source="videos/stokes_proof_idea_1080p.mp4"/>
</figure>

<p>
The theorem is most useful when one of the two sides is much easier than
the other. Reading it from left to right replaces an awkward line
integral by a flux integral, as in <xref ref="example-stokes-cylinder"/>;
reading it from right to left replaces an awkward surface integral by a
line integral around the boundary, as in
<xref ref="example-stokes-paraboloid"/>.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-stokes-hemisphere">
<title>Both Sides of Stokes' Theorem</title>
<statement>
<p>
Verify <xref ref="eq-stokes"/> for the field
<m>\mathbf{F} = -y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m> and the upper
hemisphere <m>S</m> of <m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 1</m>, oriented by its
upward unit normal.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-stokes-hemisphere">
<p>
The boundary of <m>S</m> is the unit circle <m>C</m> in the
<m>xy</m>-plane. The right-hand rule with an upward normal makes
<m>C</m> counterclockwise seen from above, so we parametrize it by
<m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \cos t\,\mathbf{i} + \sin t\,\mathbf{j}</m>,
<m>0 \le t \le 2\pi</m>. Along <m>C</m>,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\mathbf{F}\bigl(\mathbf{r}(t)\bigr)
\amp = \left\langle -\sin t,\ \cos t,\ 0 \right\rangle ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\mathbf{r}'(t)
\amp = \left\langle -\sin t,\ \cos t,\ 0 \right\rangle ,
</mrow>
<mrow>
\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{r}'
\amp = \sin^2 t + \cos^2 t = 1 ,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
so the circulation is
</p>

<me>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = \int_0^{2\pi} 1\, dt = 2\pi .
</me>

<p>
For the other side, <xref ref="eq-curl-3d"/> gives
<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = 2\,\mathbf{k}</m>, a constant field. On the
hemisphere the outward unit normal is
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = x\,\mathbf{i} + y\,\mathbf{j} + z\,\mathbf{k}</m>
by <xref ref="eq-sphere-unit-normal"/>,
so <m>(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}} = 2z</m> and
</p>

<me>
\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \iint_S 2z\, d\sigma .
</me>

<p>
Parametrizing the hemisphere by <xref ref="eq-sphere-param"/> with
<m>d\sigma = \sin\phi\, d\phi\, d\theta</m> from
<xref ref="eq-sphere-cross"/> and
<m>z = \cos\phi</m>,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\iint_S 2z\, d\sigma
\amp = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{\pi/2}
2\cos\phi \sin\phi\, d\phi\, d\theta
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \int_0^{2\pi} \Bigl[\sin^2\phi\Bigr]_0^{\pi/2} d\theta
= \int_0^{2\pi} 1\, d\theta = 2\pi ,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
which matches the circulation, as <xref ref="thm-stokes"/> promises.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-hemisphere-tikz">
<caption>
The surface and boundary of <xref ref="example-stokes-hemisphere"/>:
the upper hemisphere <m>S</m> of <m>x^2+y^2+z^2=1</m> with its upward
unit normal <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, drawn at the top and at a point on
the side, and the unit circle <m>C</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane,
counterclockwise seen from above. The field
<m>\mathbf{F} = -y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}</m> (purple arrows)
runs tangent to <m>C</m> all the way round, which is why the circulation is
as large as it can be.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-stokes-hemisphere" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A hemisphere with an upward normal, its boundary circle, and a field tangent to that circle.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent blue hemisphere sits on the horizontal plane. Its rim is
the unit circle, drawn in vermillion with arrowheads pointing
counterclockwise seen from above, and eight short purple arrows just
outside the circle show the field running tangent to it. A green arrow
rises from the top of the hemisphere, and a second green arrow points
straight out of the hemisphere from a point on its side.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The upper hemisphere S of the unit sphere with its outward (upward) unit normal, its
// boundary circle C counterclockwise seen from above, and the field F = <-y, x, 0> tangent to C.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(11cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={120}{20}).
triple cam = (0.8138, 0.4698, 0.3420);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// arrowheads along a closed curve: short pieces of it, each ending in a head
void heads(triple f(real), real[] at, pen p, real len=0.22) {
  for (real t : at) draw(graph(f, t - len, t, 12), p, Arrow3(size=13));
}

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-1.5, 0, 0)--(1.5, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -1.5, 0)--(0, 1.5, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 0, 1.7), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the hemisphere, by colatitude phi and longitude theta
triple hem(pair q) { return (sin(q.x)*cos(q.y), sin(q.x)*sin(q.y), cos(q.x)); }
draw(surface(hem, (0, 0), (pi/2, 2pi), 6, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.4*oiblue + 0.6*white + opacity(0.38),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
pen mesh = 0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + linewidth(0.4pt);
for (real ph : new real[] {pi/6, pi/3})
  draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return hem((ph, t)); }, 0, 2pi, 60), mesh);
for (int k = 0; k < 8; ++k)
  draw(graph(new triple(real ph) { return hem((ph, k*pi/4)); }, 0, pi/2, 16), mesh);

// the boundary circle C
triple C(real t) { return (cos(t), sin(t), 0); }
draw(graph(C, 0, 2pi, 120), oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));
heads(C, new real[] {0.25, 2.35, 4.45}, oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("$C$", 1.2*C(0.75), oivermillion + fontsize(18pt));

// the field F = <-y, x, 0> at eight points of C: tangent to C all the way round
// (drawn just outside C, so that the arrows are not hidden by the curve)
pen fieldpen = 0.85*oipurple + linewidth(1.3pt);
for (int k = 0; k < 8; ++k) {
  real t = pi/8 + k*pi/4;
  draw(1.07*C(t)--1.07*C(t) + 0.45*(-sin(t), cos(t), 0), fieldpen, Arrow3(size=8));
}
label("$\mathbf{F}$", 1.07*C(pi/8) + 0.5*(-sin(pi/8), cos(pi/8), 0), E, 0.85*oipurple + fontsize(16pt));

// the unit normal n-hat = <x, y, z>, at the top and at a point on the side
draw(Z--1.5*Z, oigreen + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", 1.5*Z, E, oigreen + fontsize(18pt));
triple q = hem((0.95, 2.0));
draw(q--1.45*q, oigreen + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
dot(q, oigreen + linewidth(4pt));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", 1.45*q, E, oigreen + fontsize(18pt));

label("$S$", hem((0.95, 4.55)), 2*W, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<example xml:id="example-stokes-cylinder">
<title>A Circulation That Is Easier as a Flux</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> for
<m>\mathbf{F} = -y^2\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j} + z^2\,\mathbf{k}</m>,
where <m>C</m> is the curve in which the plane <m>y + z = 2</m> cuts the
cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m>, traversed counterclockwise when seen from
above. The curve is drawn in
<xref ref="fig-stokes-cylinder-tikz"/>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-stokes-cylinder">
<p>
Parametrizing <m>C</m> and integrating directly is possible but
unpleasant. Instead take <m>S</m> to be the flat elliptical region that
<m>C</m> bounds in the plane <m>y + z = 2</m>, that is the graph of
<m>z = 2 - y</m> over the unit disk <m>R</m> in the
<m>xy</m>-plane. For a graph <m>z = g(x,y)</m> with an upward normal,
<xref ref="eq-flux-param"/> with
<m>\mathbf{r}(x,y) = \left\langle x,\ y,\ g(x,y) \right\rangle</m> and
the cross product
<m>\mathbf{r}_x \times \mathbf{r}_y
= \left\langle -g_x,\ -g_y,\ 1 \right\rangle</m>, worked out in
<xref ref="check-graph-area-element-tf"/> (there with <m>f</m> in place
of <m>g</m>), gives
</p>

<me>
\hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \left\langle -g_x,\ -g_y,\ 1 \right\rangle dx\, dy
= \left\langle 0,\ 1,\ 1 \right\rangle dx\, dy ,
</me>

<p>
and an upward normal is the one the right-hand rule pairs with a
counterclockwise <m>C</m>. From <xref ref="eq-curl-3d"/>,
</p>

<me>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F}
= \left\langle 0,\ 0,\ 1 + 2y \right\rangle ,
</me>

<p>
so <m>(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \left\langle 0, 1, 1
\right\rangle = 1 + 2y</m> and <xref ref="eq-stokes"/> turns the line
integral into a double integral over the unit disk:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
\amp = \iint_R \left(1 + 2y\right) dx\, dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \int_0^{2\pi}\!\!\int_0^{1}
\left(1 + 2r\sin\theta\right) r\, dr\, d\theta = \pi .
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The term <m>2y</m> integrates to zero because the disk is symmetric
about <m>y = 0</m>, and what is left is the area <m>\pi</m> of the disk.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-cylinder-tikz">
<caption>
The curve <m>C</m> of <xref ref="example-stokes-cylinder"/>: the plane
<m>y + z = 2</m> cuts the cylinder <m>x^2 + y^2 = 1</m> in an ellipse.
The shaded elliptical region is the surface <m>S</m> used in the
solution, with its upward normal <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> along
<m>\left\langle 0, 1, 1 \right\rangle</m>, and its shadow is the unit disk
<m>R</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-stokes-cylinder" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A vertical cylinder cut by a slanted plane, with the ellipse of
intersection and its shadow on the horizontal plane.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent vertical cylinder of radius one stands on the horizontal
plane. A slanted plane, drawn as a faint sheet a little wider than the
cylinder and labelled y plus z equals 2, cuts it in an ellipse drawn in
vermillion, with arrowheads showing counterclockwise travel seen from
above. The elliptical region inside the curve is shaded blue, and a green
arrow labelled n hat stands on it, perpendicular to the plane and pointing
upward. Directly below, the unit disk in the x y plane is shaded gray as
the shadow of the region, and four dashed vertical lines on the cylinder
join the curve to the rim of the disk.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The plane y + z = 2 cuts the cylinder x^2 + y^2 = 1 in the ellipse C; S is the elliptical
// region it bounds in the plane, with upward normal along <0, 1, 1>, and R is its shadow.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(11.5cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={115}{18}).
triple cam = (0.8619, 0.4019, 0.3090);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// arrowheads along a closed curve: short pieces of it, each ending in a head
void heads(triple f(real), real[] at, pen p, real len=0.22) {
  for (real t : at) draw(graph(f, t - len, t, 12), p, Arrow3(size=13));
}

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-1.5, 0, 0)--(1.9, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -1.9, 0)--(0, 1.9, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 0, 3.9), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the unit disk R in the xy-plane, the shadow of S
path3 unitcircle = graph(new triple(real t) { return (cos(t), sin(t), 0); }, 0, 2pi, 72) & cycle;
triple floor(pair q) { return (q.x*cos(q.y), q.x*sin(q.y), 0); }
draw(surface(floor, (0.01, 0), (1, 2pi), 2, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(gray(0.8) + opacity(0.55), emissivepen=gray(0.25)));
draw(unitcircle, gray(0.5) + linewidth(0.6pt));

// the cutting plane y + z = 2, a little beyond the cylinder
path3 plane = (-1.45, -1.45, 3.45)--(1.45, -1.45, 3.45)--(1.45, 1.45, 0.55)--(-1.45, 1.45, 0.55)--cycle;
draw(surface(plane), surfacepen=material(gray(0.7) + opacity(0.16), emissivepen=gray(0.3)));
draw(plane, gray(0.55) + linewidth(0.5pt));
label("$y+z=2$", (-1.45, -1.45, 3.45), N, gray(0.3) + fontsize(15pt));

// the cylinder wall, from the xy-plane up to the cut
triple wall(pair q) { return (cos(q.x), sin(q.x), q.y*(2 - sin(q.x))); }
draw(surface(wall, (0, 0), (2pi, 1), 24, 1, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(gray(0.75) + opacity(0.25), emissivepen=gray(0.3), specularpen=gray(0.05)));
// four generators, to tie C to its shadow
pen guide = gray(0.5) + linewidth(0.6pt) + linetype("4 3");
for (int k = 0; k < 4; ++k) draw(wall((k*pi/2, 0))--wall((k*pi/2, 1)), guide);

// the elliptical region S in the plane
// (a polar grid with a pinhole at the centre, which keeps every patch non-degenerate)
triple ell(pair q) { return (q.x*cos(q.y), q.x*sin(q.y), 2 - q.x*sin(q.y)); }
draw(surface(ell, (0.01, 0), (1, 2pi), 2, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.4*oiblue + 0.6*white + opacity(0.7),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));

// the curve C, counterclockwise seen from above
triple C(real t) { return (cos(t), sin(t), 2 - sin(t)); }
draw(graph(C, 0, 2pi, 140), oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));
heads(C, new real[] {0.6, 2.7, 4.75}, oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));
label("$C$", C(1.15) + 0.25*(cos(1.15), sin(1.15), 0), oivermillion + fontsize(18pt));

// the upward normal of S, along <0, 1, 1>
triple n = unit((0, 1, 1));
draw((0, 0, 2)--(0, 0, 2) + 0.95*n, oigreen + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", (0, 0, 2) + 0.95*n, N, oigreen + fontsize(18pt));

label("$S$", ell((0.55, 3.6)) + toviewer, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
label("$R$", (0.45, -0.35, 0) + toviewer, gray(0.2) + fontsize(18pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<!-- The next four examples are variations on the worked examples of    -->
<!-- CLP-4 Vector Calculus, Section 4.4 (Feldman, Rechnitzer, Yeager),  -->
<!-- with different fields, curves, and numbers.                        -->

<p>
The next example shows the theorem at its most merciless: one term of
the field cannot be integrated in closed form at all, and Stokes'
theorem simply erases it.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-stokes-flat-disk">
<title>A Term That Cannot Be Integrated</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> for
<me>
\mathbf{F} = \left[\cos\left(x^{100}\right) + 3y\right]\mathbf{i}
+ 4z\,\mathbf{j} + 2y\,\mathbf{k},
</me>
where <m>C</m> is the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m>, <m>z = 2</m>,
traversed counterclockwise when seen from above.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-stokes-flat-disk">
<p>
The term <m>\cos\left(x^{100}\right)</m> makes a direct evaluation
hopeless<mdash/>its antiderivative has no closed form. But Stokes'
theorem only ever sees the field through its curl, and by
<xref ref="eq-curl-3d"/>,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F}
\amp = \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(2y)
- \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(4z) \right) \mathbf{i}
+ 0\,\mathbf{j}
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad + \left( 0 - \frac{\partial}{\partial y}
\left[\cos\left(x^{100}\right) + 3y\right] \right) \mathbf{k}
= -2\,\mathbf{i} - 3\,\mathbf{k}:
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
the monster differentiates to zero. For the surface we take the
cheapest thing with boundary <m>C</m>: the flat disk
<m>S = \left\{ (x,y,z) : x^2 + y^2 \le 9,\ z = 2 \right\}</m>, whose
unit normal (paired with the counterclockwise orientation of <m>C</m>
by the right-hand rule) is <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{k}</m>. Then
<m>(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}} = -3</m> is
constant, so
</p>

<me>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
= \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= -3 \cdot \text{Area}(S) = -3 \left(9\pi\right) = -27\pi .
</me>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-flat-disk-tikz">
<caption>
The cheapest surface for <xref ref="example-stokes-flat-disk"/>: the
flat disk <m>S</m> of radius <m>3</m> in the plane <m>z=2</m>, whose
boundary is the given circle <m>C</m>. Its unit normal is
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{k}</m>, so only the
<m>\mathbf{k}</m>-component of the curl survives. The curl
<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = -2\,\mathbf{i} - 3\,\mathbf{k}</m>
(purple, drawn at one point; it is the same everywhere) points to the other
side of the disk from <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, which is why the answer is
negative.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-stokes-flat-disk" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A flat disk floating at height two, with its boundary circle and an upward normal.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent blue disk of radius three lies in the horizontal plane at
height two above the origin. Its boundary circle is drawn in vermillion with
arrowheads pointing counterclockwise seen from above, and a green arrow
labelled n hat equals k rises from the centre of the disk. A purple arrow
labelled curl F starts at another point of the disk and points down through
it and toward the negative x direction. A dashed line marks the radius 3 on
the x axis and a tick marks the height 2 on the z axis.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The flat disk S of radius 3 in the plane z = 2, its boundary circle C, its unit normal k,
// and the curl <-2, 0, -3> of the field, which points to the other side of the disk.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(11cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={118}{18}).
triple cam = (0.8397, 0.4465, 0.3090);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// arrowheads along a closed curve: short pieces of it, each ending in a head
void heads(triple f(real), real[] at, pen p, real len=0.22) {
  for (real t : at) draw(graph(f, t - len, t, 12), p, Arrow3(size=13));
}

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-4, 0, 0)--(4.4, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -4, 0)--(0, 4.4, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 0, 3.9), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the disk S at height 2
triple C(real t) { return (3*cos(t), 3*sin(t), 2); }
path3 circ = graph(C, 0, 2pi, 120) & cycle;
// (a polar grid with a pinhole at the centre, which keeps every patch non-degenerate)
triple disk(pair q) { return (q.x*cos(q.y), q.x*sin(q.y), 2); }
draw(surface(disk, (0.02, 0), (3, 2pi), 2, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.3*oiblue + 0.7*white + opacity(0.6),
                         emissivepen=0.35*oiblue + 0.35*white, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(circ, oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));
heads(C, new real[] {0.2, 2.3, 4.4}, oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt), 0.16);
label("$C$", (3.4*cos(0.75), 3.4*sin(0.75), 2), oivermillion + fontsize(18pt));
label("$S$", (-1.4, 1.5, 2) + toviewer, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));

// its radius and its height
pen guide = gray(0.5) + linewidth(0.6pt) + linetype("4 3");
draw((3, 0, 0)--(3, 0, 2), guide);
label("$3$", (3, 0, 0), S, fontsize(13pt));
draw((0, -0.1, 2)--(0, 0.1, 2), gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt));
label("$2$", (0, -0.1, 2), W, fontsize(13pt));

// the unit normal the right-hand rule pairs with C
draw((0, 0, 2)--(0, 0, 3.15), oigreen + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}=\mathbf{k}$", (0, 0, 3.15), E, oigreen + fontsize(16pt));

// the curl of F, the same at every point: -2 i - 3 k (drawn at 0.4 of its length)
triple A = (2.5, 0.9, 2);                  // near the front edge, so that the arrow shows below the disk
triple curlF = 0.4*(-2, 0, -3);
draw(A--A + curlF, oipurple + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
dot(A, oipurple + linewidth(4pt));
label("$\nabla\times\mathbf{F}$", A + curlF, E, oipurple + fontsize(16pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
The same trick works when the curve is tilted<mdash/>the flat surface it
bounds just tilts with it.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-stokes-tilted-circle">
<title>A Tilted Circle</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> for
<me>
\mathbf{F} = \ln\left(1 + x^8\right)\mathbf{i} - z\,\mathbf{j}
+ y\,\mathbf{k},
</me>
where <m>C</m> is the intersection of the sphere
<m>x^2 + y^2 + z^2 = 9</m> with the plane <m>z = x</m>, traversed
counterclockwise when seen from above.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-stokes-tilted-circle">
<p>
The plane <m>z = x</m> passes through the centre of the sphere, so
<m>C</m> is a great circle: centre <m>(0,0,0)</m> and radius <m>3</m>.
The <m>\ln\left(1 + x^8\right)</m> again rules out a direct attack, and
again it dies under the curl: by <xref ref="eq-curl-3d"/>,
</p>

<me>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F}
= \left( \frac{\partial}{\partial y}(y)
- \frac{\partial}{\partial z}(-z) \right) \mathbf{i}
+ 0\,\mathbf{j} + 0\,\mathbf{k}
= 2\,\mathbf{i} .
</me>

<p>
Take <m>S</m> to be the flat disk that <m>C</m> bounds in the plane
<m>z = x</m>. The gradient of <m>z - x</m> is
<m>-\mathbf{i} + \mathbf{k}</m>, so the upward unit normal of <m>S</m>
(the one the right-hand rule pairs with counterclockwise-from-above) is
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}(-\mathbf{i} + \mathbf{k})</m>,
and the integrand
</p>

<me>
(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}
= 2\,\mathbf{i} \cdot
\frac{-\mathbf{i} + \mathbf{k}}{\sqrt{2}}
= -\sqrt{2}
</me>

<p>
is constant. Since <m>S</m> is a disk of radius <m>3</m>,
</p>

<me>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
= -\sqrt{2}\ \text{Area}(S)
= -\sqrt{2}\left(9\pi\right)
= -9\sqrt{2}\,\pi .
</me>

<p>
If the constant-integrand shortcut goes unnoticed, the standard graph
protocol still works: on <m>S</m>, <m>z = f(x,y) = x</m>, so
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \left\langle -f_x, -f_y, 1 \right\rangle dx\,dy
= \left\langle -1, 0, 1 \right\rangle dx\,dy</m>
and
<m>(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= -2\, dx\,dy</m>. Substituting <m>z = x</m> into the sphere,
the shadow of <m>S</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane is the ellipse
<m>2x^2 + y^2 \le 9</m>, with semiaxes <m>3/\sqrt{2}</m> and <m>3</m>
and area <m>9\pi/\sqrt{2}</m>. Then
<m>\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
= -2 \left( 9\pi/\sqrt{2} \right) = -9\sqrt{2}\,\pi</m>, as before.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-tilted-tikz">
<caption>
The curve of <xref ref="example-stokes-tilted-circle"/>: the plane
<m>z=x</m> passes through the centre of the sphere
<m>x^2+y^2+z^2=9</m>, so it cuts out a great circle <m>C</m> of radius
<m>3</m>. The surface <m>S</m> is the flat disk that <m>C</m> bounds in that
plane, tilted at <m>45^\circ</m>, and its unit normal is
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \left(-\mathbf{i}+\mathbf{k}\right)/\sqrt2</m>.
The curl <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = 2\,\mathbf{i}</m> (purple) makes
an angle of more than <m>90^\circ</m> with <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, so
the flux, and with it the circulation, is negative.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-stokes-tilted" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A sphere cut by a slanted plane through its centre, with the great circle and the tilted disk it bounds.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A faint grey sphere of radius three is cut through its centre by a
slanted plane. The circle of intersection is drawn in vermillion with
arrowheads showing counterclockwise travel seen from above, the flat disk
inside it is shaded blue, and a green arrow perpendicular to that disk
points up and toward the negative x direction. A purple arrow from the
centre, the curl of F, points along the positive x axis.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The plane z = x cuts the sphere of radius 3 in a great circle C; S is the flat disk it
// bounds, n-hat = (-i + k)/sqrt 2 its upward unit normal, and the curl of F is 2 i.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(11cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={118}{16}).
triple cam = (0.8487, 0.4513, 0.2756);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// arrowheads along a closed curve: short pieces of it, each ending in a head
void heads(triple f(real), real[] at, pen p, real len=0.22) {
  for (real t : at) draw(graph(f, t - len, t, 12), p, Arrow3(size=13));
}

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-4, 0, 0)--(4.6, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -4, 0)--(0, 4.4, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, -3.6)--(0, 0, 3.9), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the sphere of radius 3, faint
triple sph(pair q) { return 3*(sin(q.x)*cos(q.y), sin(q.x)*sin(q.y), cos(q.x)); }
draw(surface(sph, (0, 0), (pi, 2pi), 12, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(gray(0.8) + opacity(0.14), emissivepen=gray(0.3), specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return 3*(cos(t), sin(t), 0); }, 0, 2pi, 90), gray(0.7) + linewidth(0.4pt));
label("$x^2+y^2+z^2=9$", sph((0.75, -2.0)), W, gray(0.35) + fontsize(14pt));

// the disk S in the plane z = x, spanned by (1,0,1)/sqrt 2 and (0,1,0)
real h = sqrt(0.5);
triple C(real t) { return 3*(h*cos(t), sin(t), h*cos(t)); }
path3 circ = graph(C, 0, 2pi, 140) & cycle;
// (a polar grid with a pinhole at the centre, which keeps every patch non-degenerate)
// (the angle runs backwards so that the side facing the viewer is the lit one)
triple disk(pair q) { return q.x*(h*cos(q.y), -sin(q.y), h*cos(q.y)); }
draw(surface(disk, (0.02, 0), (3, 2pi), 2, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.3*oiblue + 0.7*white + opacity(0.6),
                         emissivepen=0.35*oiblue + 0.35*white, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(circ, oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));
heads(C, new real[] {0.2, 2.3, 4.4}, oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt), 0.16);
label("$C$", 1.13*C(0.8), oivermillion + fontsize(18pt));
label("$S$", 0.55*C(1.25) + toviewer, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
label("plane $z=x$", 1.05*C(2.25), E, oiblue + fontsize(14pt));

// the upward unit normal of S and the curl of F, both drawn from the centre
triple n = (-h, 0, h);
draw(O--1.6*n, oigreen + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", 1.6*n, N, oigreen + fontsize(18pt));
draw(O--(2, 0, 0), oipurple + linewidth(1.8pt), Arrow3(size=10));
label("$\nabla\times\mathbf{F}$", (2.2, 0, 0) + 0.6*unit(cam), 2*S, 0.8*oipurple + fontsize(15pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
When the boundary is made of straight segments, both sides of
<xref ref="eq-stokes"/> are computable, and the comparison is
instructive: the direct route costs three parametrizations, the Stokes
route one double integral.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-stokes-triangle">
<title>Around a Triangle, Twice</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> for
<me>
\mathbf{F} = (y - z)\,\mathbf{i} + (x + z)\,\mathbf{j}
+ \left(x^2 + y\right)\mathbf{k},
</me>
where <m>C</m> goes from <m>(2,0,0)</m> to <m>(0,2,0)</m> to
<m>(0,0,4)</m> and back to <m>(2,0,0)</m> along straight line segments,
as in <xref ref="fig-stokes-triangle-tikz"/>. Do the computation twice:
directly, and by Stokes' theorem.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-stokes-triangle">
<p>
<em>Directly.</em> The segment <m>C_1</m> from <m>(2,0,0)</m> to
<m>(0,2,0)</m> is <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = (2-2t,\ 2t,\ 0)</m>,
<m>0 \le t \le 1</m>, with
<m>\mathbf{r}'(t) = (-2,\ 2,\ 0)</m>, so
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\int_{C_1} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
\amp = \int_0^1 \left(2t,\ 2-2t,\ (2-2t)^2 + 2t\right) \cdot (-2,\ 2,\ 0)\, dt
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \int_0^1 (4 - 8t)\, dt = 0 .
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The segment <m>C_2</m> from <m>(0,2,0)</m> to <m>(0,0,4)</m> is
<m>\mathbf{r}(t) = (0,\ 2-2t,\ 4t)</m> with
<m>\mathbf{r}'(t) = (0,\ -2,\ 4)</m>, so
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\int_{C_2} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
\amp = \int_0^1 \left(2 - 6t,\ 4t,\ 2 - 2t\right) \cdot (0,\ -2,\ 4)\, dt
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \int_0^1 (8 - 16t)\, dt = 0 ,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
and the segment <m>C_3</m> from <m>(0,0,4)</m> back to <m>(2,0,0)</m>
is <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = (2t,\ 0,\ 4-4t)</m> with
<m>\mathbf{r}'(t) = (2,\ 0,\ -4)</m>, so
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\int_{C_3} \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
\amp = \int_0^1 \left(4t - 4,\ 4 - 2t,\ 4t^2\right) \cdot (2,\ 0,\ -4)\, dt
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \int_0^1 \left(8t - 8 - 16t^2\right) dt = -\frac{28}{3} .
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The total is <m>0 + 0 - \frac{28}{3} = -\frac{28}{3}</m>.
</p>

<p>
<em>By Stokes' theorem.</em> By <xref ref="eq-curl-3d"/>,
<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = (1-1)\,\mathbf{i}
+ (-1 - 2x)\,\mathbf{j} + (1-1)\,\mathbf{k}
= -(1 + 2x)\,\mathbf{j}</m>. The triangle lies in the plane
<m>\frac{x}{2} + \frac{y}{2} + \frac{z}{4} = 1</m>, that is, the graph
of <m>z = 4 - 2x - 2y</m> over the shadow triangle
<m>R = \left\{ (x,y) : x \ge 0,\ y \ge 0,\ x + y \le 2 \right\}</m>.
The traversal <m>(2,0,0) \to (0,2,0) \to (0,0,4)</m> is
counterclockwise seen from above, which pairs with the upward normal
<m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \left\langle 2,\ 2,\ 1 \right\rangle dx\,dy</m>. So
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
\amp = \iint_R \left\langle 0,\ -(1+2x),\ 0 \right\rangle \cdot
\left\langle 2,\ 2,\ 1 \right\rangle dx\,dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = \iint_R (-2 - 4x)\, dx\,dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp = -2\,\text{Area}(R) - 4 \iint_R x\, dx\,dy
= -2(2) - 4\left(\frac{4}{3}\right)
= -\frac{28}{3} ,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
using <m>\iint_R x\, dx\,dy = \int_0^2 x(2-x)\, dx = \frac{4}{3}</m>.
The two answers agree, and the second computation is the one to
remember.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-triangle-tikz">
<caption>
The oriented triangle of <xref ref="example-stokes-triangle"/>: from
<m>(2,0,0)</m> to <m>(0,2,0)</m> to <m>(0,0,4)</m> and back, bounding a
piece <m>S</m> of the plane
<m>\frac{x}{2} + \frac{y}{2} + \frac{z}{4} = 1</m>. The three edges are
the segments <m>C_1</m>, <m>C_2</m>, <m>C_3</m> of the direct computation.
The traversal is counterclockwise seen from above, pairing with the
upward normal <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m> by the right-hand rule, and the gray
triangle <m>R</m> in the <m>xy</m>-plane is the shadow of <m>S</m>.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-stokes-triangle" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A triangle in space with vertices on the three positive coordinate
axes, its edges carrying arrowheads that circle counterclockwise seen
from above.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
Coordinate axes in three dimensions. A blue triangular plate has its
corners on the positive x axis at 2, the positive y axis at 2, and the
positive z axis at 4. Vermillion arrows run along its edges from the x
axis corner to the y axis corner, labelled C 1, then up to the z axis
corner, labelled C 2, then back down to the x axis corner, labelled C 3, a
circuit that is counterclockwise when seen from above. A green arrow
labelled n hat stands on the plate, perpendicular to it and pointing up and
away from the origin. Below the plate, the gray triangle in the x y plane
with corners at the origin and at 2 on the x and y axes is its shadow,
labelled R.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The oriented triangle (2,0,0) -> (0,2,0) -> (0,0,4) -> (2,0,0): its three edges C1, C2, C3,
// the plate S they bound with its upward normal along <2, 2, 1>, and the shadow R of S.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(10.5cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={103}{14}).
triple cam = (0.9454, 0.2183, 0.2419);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(3.2, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 2.9, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 0, 5.1), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

triple A = (2, 0, 0), B = (0, 2, 0), D = (0, 0, 4);

// the shadow R of the plate in the xy-plane
path3 shadow = O--A--B--cycle;
draw(surface(shadow), surfacepen=material(gray(0.8) + opacity(0.6), emissivepen=gray(0.25)));
label("$R$", (0.55, 0.55, 0) + toviewer, gray(0.2) + fontsize(18pt));

// the triangular plate S
path3 plate = A--B--D--cycle;
draw(surface(plate), surfacepen=material(0.4*oiblue + 0.6*white + opacity(0.6),
                                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));

// the three oriented edges, each with a head at its midpoint
pen edge = oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt);
void side(triple p, triple q) {
  draw(p--q, edge);
  draw(p--(0.58*q + 0.42*p), edge, Arrow3(size=13));
}
side(A, B); side(B, D); side(D, A);
label("$C_1$", (A + B)/2 + toviewer, 2*S, oivermillion + fontsize(16pt));
label("$C_2$", (B + D)/2, 2*E, oivermillion + fontsize(16pt));
label("$C_3$", (D + A)/2, 2*W, oivermillion + fontsize(16pt));

dot(A, linewidth(4pt)); dot(B, linewidth(4pt)); dot(D, linewidth(4pt));
label("$(2,0,0)$", A, 2*W, fontsize(13pt));
label("$(0,2,0)$", B, 2*S + E, fontsize(13pt));
label("$(0,0,4)$", D, 2*W, fontsize(13pt));

// the upward normal of S, along <2, 2, 1>, from the centroid
triple G = (A + B + D)/3;
triple n = unit((2, 2, 1));
draw(G--G + 1.5*n, oigreen + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
dot(G, oigreen + linewidth(4pt));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", G + 1.5*n, N, oigreen + fontsize(18pt));
label("$S$", (0.25*A + 0.3*B + 0.45*D) + toviewer, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
Finally, before reaching for Stokes' theorem at all, it pays to glance
at the curl: if it vanishes, no surface is needed.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-stokes-conservative">
<title>The Curve Nobody Can Parametrize</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate <m>\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> for
<me>
\mathbf{F} = \left(e^x + y + z\right)\mathbf{i}
+ (x + z)\,\mathbf{j} + (x + y)\,\mathbf{k},
</me>
where <m>C</m> is the intersection of the ellipsoid
<m>x^2 + \frac{y^2}{4} + \frac{z^2}{9} = 1</m> with the paraboloid
<m>z = 2x^2 + y^2</m>, traversed counterclockwise when seen from above.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-stokes-conservative">
<p>
The curve is a warped loop around the paraboloid<mdash/>solving the two
equations simultaneously for a parametrization would be miserable, and
whatever surface it bounds is worse. But by <xref ref="eq-curl-3d"/>,
</p>

<me>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F}
= (1 - 1)\,\mathbf{i} + (1 - 1)\,\mathbf{j} + (1 - 1)\,\mathbf{k}
= \mathbf{0} .
</me>

<p>
By the component test <xref ref="eq-component-test-3d"/>, this
<m>\mathbf{F}</m> is conservative<mdash/>indeed
<m>\mathbf{F} = \nabla\!\left(e^x + xy + xz + yz\right)</m><mdash/>so
whichever surface <m>S</m> the loop bounds,
</p>

<me>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
= \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \iint_S \mathbf{0} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= 0 ,
</me>

<p>
and the geometry of <m>C</m> never enters. Closed curve, conservative
field, zero work<mdash/>exactly as in
<xref ref="section-conservative-fields"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-warped-loop-tikz">
<caption>
The curve of <xref ref="example-stokes-conservative"/>: the paraboloid
<m>z = 2x^2+y^2</m> meets the ellipsoid
<m>x^2+\frac{y^2}{4}+\frac{z^2}{9}=1</m> in a warped loop <m>C</m>. Nothing
about its shape matters here: the curl of <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is
<m>\mathbf{0}</m>, so the circulation around it is zero whatever surface it
bounds.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-stokes-warped-loop" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A warped closed curve where a paraboloid meets an ellipsoid.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent blue paraboloid opening upward passes through a faint
grey ellipsoid. They meet along a closed loop, drawn in vermillion with
arrowheads showing counterclockwise travel seen from above. The loop is not
flat: it rises and falls as it goes round.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// The paraboloid z = 2x^2 + y^2 meets the ellipsoid x^2 + y^2/4 + z^2/9 = 1 in a warped loop C,
// traversed counterclockwise seen from above.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(11cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={122}{18}).
triple cam = (0.8065, 0.5040, 0.3090);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// arrowheads along a closed curve: short pieces of it, each ending in a head
void heads(triple f(real), real[] at, pen p, real len=0.22) {
  for (real t : at) draw(graph(f, t - len, t, 12), p, Arrow3(size=13));
}

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-2.2, 0, 0)--(2.4, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -2.6, 0)--(0, 2.8, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 0, 3.6), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the ellipsoid, upper half, faint
triple ell(pair q) { return (sin(q.x)*cos(q.y), 2*sin(q.x)*sin(q.y), 3*cos(q.x)); }
draw(surface(ell, (0, 0), (pi/2, 2pi), 8, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(gray(0.8) + opacity(0.2), emissivepen=gray(0.3), specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return ell((pi/2, t)); }, 0, 2pi, 90), gray(0.65) + linewidth(0.4pt));

// the paraboloid z = 2x^2 + y^2, up to the height 2.7
real top = 2.7;
triple par(pair q) {
  real r = q.x*sqrt(top/(2*cos(q.y)^2 + sin(q.y)^2));
  real x = r*cos(q.y), y = r*sin(q.y);
  return (x, y, 2*x^2 + y^2);
}
draw(surface(par, (0, 0), (1, 2pi), 8, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.4*oiblue + 0.6*white + opacity(0.4),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return par((1, t)); }, 0, 2pi, 90), 0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + linewidth(0.5pt));

// the loop C: on the ray at angle t, x^2 + y^2 = s solves
//   s (cos^2 t + sin^2 t / 4) + s^2 (2 cos^2 t + sin^2 t)^2 / 9 = 1
triple C(real t) {
  real a = cos(t)^2 + sin(t)^2/4, b = (2*cos(t)^2 + sin(t)^2)^2/9;
  real s = (-a + sqrt(a^2 + 4*b))/(2*b);
  real x = sqrt(s)*cos(t), y = sqrt(s)*sin(t);
  return (x, y, 2*x^2 + y^2);
}
draw(graph(C, 0, 2pi, 160), oivermillion + linewidth(1.8pt));
heads(C, new real[] {0.75, 2.6, 4.45}, oivermillion + linewidth(1.8pt));
label("$C$", C(1.57) + (0, 0.42, 0.05), oivermillion + fontsize(18pt));

label("$z=2x^2+y^2$", par((0.45, 1.5)), 3*E, oiblue + fontsize(14pt));
label("$x^2+\frac{y^2}{4}+\frac{z^2}{9}=1$", ell((1.25, -1.9)), W, gray(0.35) + fontsize(14pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>
</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-stokes-surface-independence">
<title>Only the Boundary Matters</title>

<p>
The right-hand side of <xref ref="eq-stokes"/> mentions <m>S</m>, but the
left-hand side does not. So if two oriented surfaces <m>S_1</m> and
<m>S_2</m> share the same boundary curve <m>C</m>, with orientations that
give the same direction on <m>C</m>, both flux integrals equal the same
circulation:
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-stokes-independence">
\iint_{S_1} (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
= \iint_{S_2} (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\,
d\sigma .
</men>

<p>
This is the surface analogue of the path independence of
<xref ref="section-conservative-fields"/>, and it has the same practical
use: when a surface is unpleasant, trade it for any other surface with
the same boundary. It also shows that a curl field has no net flux through a closed
surface. Gluing
<m>S_1</m> and <m>S_2</m> along <m>C</m> makes a closed surface; the two
flux integrals appear with opposite signs once both normals point
outward, so the total flux of <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m> through any
closed surface is zero.
</p>

<example xml:id="example-stokes-paraboloid">
<title>Trading a Paraboloid for a Disk</title>
<statement>
<p>
Evaluate
<m>\displaystyle\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot
\hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</m> for
<m>\mathbf{F} = y\,\mathbf{i} + z\,\mathbf{j} + x\,\mathbf{k}</m>,
where <m>S</m> is the part of the paraboloid
<m>z = 4 - x^2 - y^2</m> above the <m>xy</m>-plane, oriented upward.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution xml:id="solution-stokes-paraboloid">
<p>
The boundary of <m>S</m> is the circle <m>x^2 + y^2 = 4</m> in the plane
<m>z = 0</m>, and the upward orientation makes it counterclockwise seen
from above. By <xref ref="eq-stokes-independence"/> we may replace the
paraboloid by the flat disk <m>S_2</m> of radius <m>2</m> in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, oriented upward, which has the same boundary. On that
disk <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}} = \mathbf{k}</m> and <m>d\sigma = dx\,dy</m>.
</p>

<p>
From <xref ref="eq-curl-3d"/>,
<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = -\mathbf{i} - \mathbf{j} -
\mathbf{k}</m>, so
<m>(\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \mathbf{k} = -1</m> and
</p>

<me>
\iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{F}) \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma
= \iint_{S_2} (-1)\, dx\, dy
= -\left(\text{area of } S_2\right) = -4\pi .
</me>

<p>
As a check, the circulation around the boundary gives the same number.
With <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = 2\cos t\,\mathbf{i} + 2\sin t\,\mathbf{j}</m>
and <m>z = 0</m> we have
<m>\mathbf{F} = \left\langle 2\sin t,\ 0,\ 2\cos t \right\rangle</m> and
<m>\mathbf{r}'(t) = \left\langle -2\sin t,\ 2\cos t,\ 0
\right\rangle</m>, so
</p>

<me>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
= \int_0^{2\pi} \left(-4\sin^2 t\right) dt = -4\pi .
</me>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-stokes-paraboloid-tikz">
<caption>
The two surfaces of <xref ref="example-stokes-paraboloid"/>: the
paraboloid <m>S</m> and the flat disk <m>S_2</m>, which share the boundary
circle <m>C</m> and carry orientations, each shown by its upward unit
normal <m>\hat{\mathbf{n}}</m>, that give the same direction on it.
By <xref ref="eq-stokes-independence"/> the flux of the curl through them is
the same, and the disk is the easy one.
</caption>
<image xml:id="image-stokes-paraboloid" width="62%">
<shortdescription>
A paraboloid and a flat disk sharing the same boundary circle.
</shortdescription>
<description>
<p>
A translucent blue paraboloid opens downward from height four to the
horizontal plane, where its rim is a circle of radius two drawn in
vermillion with arrowheads pointing counterclockwise seen from above. The
flat green disk bounded by that same circle lies in the plane. A blue
arrow labelled n hat points up and out of the paraboloid from a point on its
side, and a green arrow labelled n hat equals k rises straight up from the
disk.
</p>
</description>
<asymptote>
<![CDATA[// Two surfaces with the same boundary circle C: the paraboloid S (z = 4 - x^2 - y^2 above the
// xy-plane) and the flat disk S2, each with its upward unit normal.
import graph3;
if(settings.outformat != "html") settings.render=0;  // vector PDF, no OpenGL

size(11cm);
// The viewpoint of the pgfplots figure this replaces (view={118}{20}).
triple cam = (0.8297, 0.4412, 0.3420);
currentprojection = orthographic(cam);
currentlight = Headlamp;
currentlight.background = white;

// Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
pen oiblue       = rgb(0/255, 114/255, 178/255);
pen oivermillion = rgb(213/255, 94/255, 0/255);
pen oigreen      = rgb(0/255, 158/255, 115/255);
pen oipurple     = rgb(204/255, 121/255, 167/255);
pen axispen = gray(0.25) + linewidth(0.7pt);
triple toviewer = 0.08*unit(cam);

// arrowheads along a closed curve: short pieces of it, each ending in a head
void heads(triple f(real), real[] at, pen p, real len=0.22) {
  for (real t : at) draw(graph(f, t - len, t, 12), p, Arrow3(size=13));
}

// axes
draw(Label("$x$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (-3, 0, 0)--(3.3, 0, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$y$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, -3, 0)--(0, 3.3, 0), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));
draw(Label("$z$", EndPoint, fontsize(18pt)), (0, 0, 0)--(0, 0, 5.2), axispen, Arrow3(size=8));

// the flat disk S2 in the xy-plane
triple C(real t) { return (2*cos(t), 2*sin(t), 0); }
path3 circ = graph(C, 0, 2pi, 120) & cycle;
// (a polar grid with a pinhole at the centre, which keeps every patch non-degenerate)
triple disk(pair q) { return (q.x*cos(q.y), q.x*sin(q.y), 0); }
draw(surface(disk, (0.02, 0), (2, 2pi), 2, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.4*oigreen + 0.6*white + opacity(0.55),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oigreen, specularpen=gray(0.05)));

// the paraboloid S
triple par(pair q) { return (q.x*cos(q.y), q.x*sin(q.y), 4 - q.x^2); }
draw(surface(par, (0, 0), (2, 2pi), 8, 24, Spline),
     surfacepen=material(0.4*oiblue + 0.6*white + opacity(0.3),
                         emissivepen=0.3*oiblue, specularpen=gray(0.05)));
pen mesh = 0.5*oiblue + 0.5*white + linewidth(0.4pt);
for (real r : new real[] {0.67, 1.33})
  draw(graph(new triple(real t) { return par((r, t)); }, 0, 2pi, 60), mesh);
for (int k = 0; k < 8; ++k)
  draw(graph(new triple(real r) { return par((r, k*pi/4)); }, 0, 2, 16), mesh);

// the shared boundary C, counterclockwise seen from above
draw(circ, oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt));
heads(C, new real[] {0.2, 2.3, 4.4}, oivermillion + linewidth(1.6pt), 0.2);
label("$C$", 1.18*C(0.75), oivermillion + fontsize(18pt));

// the upward unit normal of each surface
triple p = par((1.1, 2.0));                       // on the side seen in profile
triple n = unit((2*p.x, 2*p.y, 1));
draw(p--p + 0.95*n, oiblue + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
dot(p, oiblue + linewidth(4pt));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}$", p + 0.95*n, E, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
triple q = (0.3, -1.25, 0);
draw(q--q + 0.9*Z, 0.8*oigreen + linewidth(1.6pt), Arrow3(size=10));
dot(q, 0.8*oigreen + linewidth(4pt));
label("$\hat{\mathbf{n}}=\mathbf{k}$", q + 0.9*Z, N, 0.8*oigreen + fontsize(16pt));

label("$S$", par((1.45, 4.5)), 2*W, oiblue + fontsize(18pt));
label("$S_2$", (0.9, 0.9, 0) + toviewer, 0.75*oigreen + fontsize(18pt));
]]>
</asymptote>
</image>
</figure>
</subsection>

<subsection xml:id="sec-stokes-conceptual">
<title>A Conceptual Stokes' Theorem Problem</title>

<p>
The following problem uses no computation at all. It tests whether you
can see what Stokes' theorem is really saying about circulation, curl,
and the role played by the domain of the vector field.
</p>

<exercise xml:id="ex-stokes-conceptual">
<title>Equal Curls, Equal Circulations</title>
<statement>
<p>
Let <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and <m>\mathbf{G}</m> be continuously
differentiable vector fields defined on all of <m>\R^3</m>. Suppose
that
</p>
<me>
\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = \nabla \times \mathbf{G}
</me>
<p>
everywhere in <m>\R^3</m>. Without computing any line integrals,
determine whether the statement
</p>
<me>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
= \oint_C \mathbf{G} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
</me>
<p>
is true for every smooth simple closed curve <m>C</m> in <m>\R^3</m>.
If the statement is true, prove it using Stokes' theorem,
<xref ref="thm-stokes"/>. If it is false, provide a counterexample.
</p>
</statement>
<solution>
<p>
The statement is true. Define
</p>
<me>
\mathbf{H} = \mathbf{F} - \mathbf{G}.
</me>
<p>
We first verify carefully that curl distributes over subtraction.
Write
</p>
<me>
\mathbf{F} = M_1\,\mathbf{i} + N_1\,\mathbf{j} + P_1\,\mathbf{k},
\qquad
\mathbf{G} = M_2\,\mathbf{i} + N_2\,\mathbf{j} + P_2\,\mathbf{k}.
</me>
<p>
Then
</p>
<me>
\mathbf{F} - \mathbf{G} = (M_1 - M_2)\,\mathbf{i}
+ (N_1 - N_2)\,\mathbf{j} + (P_1 - P_2)\,\mathbf{k}.
</me>
<p>
By the component formula for the curl from
<xref ref="sec-curl-3d" text="title"/>, the
<m>\mathbf{i}</m>-component of
<m>\nabla \times (\mathbf{F} - \mathbf{G})</m> is
</p>
<md>
<mrow>\frac{\partial (P_1 - P_2)}{\partial y}
- \frac{\partial (N_1 - N_2)}{\partial z}
\amp = \left( \frac{\partial P_1}{\partial y}
- \frac{\partial P_2}{\partial y} \right)
- \left( \frac{\partial N_1}{\partial z}
- \frac{\partial N_2}{\partial z} \right)</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \left( \frac{\partial P_1}{\partial y}
- \frac{\partial N_1}{\partial z} \right)
- \left( \frac{\partial P_2}{\partial y}
- \frac{\partial N_2}{\partial z} \right),</mrow>
</md>
<p>
using the linearity of partial differentiation, and the
<m>\mathbf{j}</m>- and <m>\mathbf{k}</m>-components behave the same
way. Therefore
</p>
<me>
\nabla \times (\mathbf{F} - \mathbf{G})
= \nabla \times \mathbf{F} - \nabla \times \mathbf{G},
</me>
<p>
and since <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = \nabla \times \mathbf{G}</m>,
it follows that
</p>
<me>
\nabla \times \mathbf{H}
= \nabla \times (\mathbf{F} - \mathbf{G}) = \mathbf{0}.
</me>
<p>
Now let <m>S</m> be a smooth oriented surface whose boundary is
<m>C</m>, with the direction of travel along <m>C</m> given by the
right-hand rule as in <xref ref="thm-stokes"/>. Because the fields are
defined on all of <m>\R^3</m>, the surface <m>S</m> lies entirely in
their domain. Stokes' theorem gives
</p>
<md>
<mrow>\oint_C \mathbf{H} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
\amp = \iint_S (\nabla \times \mathbf{H}) \cdot
\hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \iint_S \mathbf{0} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{n}}\, d\sigma</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = 0.</mrow>
</md>
<p>
By linearity of the line integral,
</p>
<md>
<mrow>0 \amp = \oint_C (\mathbf{F} - \mathbf{G}) \cdot d\mathbf{r}</mrow>
<mrow>\amp = \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
- \oint_C \mathbf{G} \cdot d\mathbf{r}.</mrow>
</md>
<p>
Hence
</p>
<me>
\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
= \oint_C \mathbf{G} \cdot d\mathbf{r}.
</me>
<p>
<term>Why the domain matters.</term> The crucial point is not merely
that the two curls agree. To apply Stokes' theorem, the curve <m>C</m>
must bound an oriented surface that lies entirely inside the common
domain of the vector fields. This is automatic here because the domain
is all of <m>\R^3</m>, but it can fail in a domain with a hole.
</p>
<p>
<term>Equivalent viewpoint.</term> The field
<m>\mathbf{H} = \mathbf{F} - \mathbf{G}</m> has zero curl on the
simply connected domain <m>\R^3</m>, so <m>\mathbf{H}</m> is
conservative. Therefore every closed-loop integral of <m>\mathbf{H}</m>
is zero by the loop property of
<xref ref="section-conservative-fields" text="title"/>, which gives the
same conclusion.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-stokes-concept-check">
<title>Concept Check</title>

<exercise xml:id="check-stokes-curl-conservative">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement>
<p>
Decide whether the statement is true or false, and justify your answer.
</p>
<p>
A vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> whose components have continuous first
partial derivatives on all of <m>\R^3</m> is conservative exactly when
<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = \mathbf{0}</m> everywhere.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
True.
</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
Write <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j} + P\,\mathbf{k}</m>.
The three components of <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m> are
<m>P_y - N_z</m>, <m>M_z - P_x</m> and <m>N_x - M_y</m>, so
<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = \mathbf{0}</m> says exactly that the
three equations of the component test hold, the last of them being
<xref ref="eq-component-test-3d"/>. Since <m>\R^3</m> is open and simply
connected, that test says <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is conservative if and only
if they hold. In particular, if <m>\mathbf{F} = \nabla f</m> then
<m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F} = \mathbf{0}</m>: the curl of a gradient
is zero, by the equality of the mixed partial derivatives of <m>f</m>.
</p>
<p>
The domain matters. On <m>\R^3</m> with the <m>z</m>-axis removed, which
is not simply connected, the field
<m>\mathbf{F} = \dfrac{-y\,\mathbf{i} + x\,\mathbf{j}}{x^2+y^2}</m>
has zero curl but circulation <m>2\pi</m> around the unit circle in the
<m>xy</m>-plane, so it is not conservative there
(<xref ref="section-conservative-fields"/>).
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="check-stokes-curl-flux">
<title>True or False</title>
<statement>
<p>
Decide whether the statement is true or false, and justify your answer.
</p>
<p>
There is a vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m>, whose components have
continuous second partial derivatives on all of <m>\R^3</m>, such that
the flux of <m>\nabla \times \mathbf{F}</m> outward through the unit
sphere is not zero.
</p>
</statement>
<answer>
<p>
False.
</p>
</answer>
<solution>
<p>
With <m>\mathbf{F} = M\,\mathbf{i} + N\,\mathbf{j} + P\,\mathbf{k}</m>,
</p>
<md>
<mrow>
\amp \nabla \cdot (\nabla \times \mathbf{F})
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = (P_y - N_z)_x + (M_z - P_x)_y + (N_x - M_y)_z
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp \quad = 0,
</mrow>
</md>
<p>
because the mixed partial derivatives of each component are equal and
the six terms cancel in pairs. A curl field has no divergence, so by
<xref ref="thm-divergence"/> its flux outward through the unit sphere,
and through any closed surface, is the triple integral of <m>0</m> over
the solid inside, which is <m>0</m>.
</p>
<p>
Stokes' theorem gives the same answer. Cut the sphere along the equator
<m>C</m> into two hemispheres. With both normals pointing outward, the
right-hand rule sends <m>C</m> in opposite directions as the boundary of
the two hemispheres, so by <xref ref="thm-stokes"/> their two fluxes are
circulations around <m>C</m> of opposite sign, and they cancel.
</p>
</solution>
</exercise>

</subsection>

</section>
